# vlbaweather: environment for make_vlba_page.py, the VLBA antenna weather page (every
# WEATHER_* variable the page honours and every VLBA_* variable read by vlba/config.py, each
# with its default). Format is systemd EnvironmentFile: KEY=value lines, no `export`, no
# surrounding quotes, `#` comments (the cron wrapper also tolerates blanks around the `=`).
# Command-line flags (--out, --cache, --sites, --verbose) override these.
#
# vlbaweather is built on the weather package of local-weather-awareness (copied into
# weather/), so the generic settings keep their WEATHER_* names and meanings, with the
# defaults the VLBA page changes shown here. Only the VLBA page's own settings (the verdict,
# the GLM lightning layer, the radar outside the MRMS grid, the radar coverage) are VLBA_*.
#
# The systemd unit (deploy/install.sh) reads only /etc/vlbaweather.env, as EnvironmentFile=.
# The cron wrapper (deploy/vlbaweather-cron.sh, for hosts without systemd such as
# Gentoo/OpenRC) reads /etc/vlbaweather.env, then ~<run user>/vlbaweather.env (later wins),
# parsing them literally before every run, never sourcing them. It skips, with a log line, a
# file not owned by root or the run user, or writable by group or others (the file, its link
# target or their directories), and stops (exit status 2) at one that exists but cannot be
# read. On a host that also runs local-weather-awareness the env files of the two services
# are SEPARATE: vlbaweather never reads /etc/local-weather-awareness.env, and that service
# never reads these, so a value you want in both (the User-Agent, say) goes into both files.
# Install with (as root; deploy/install-cron.sh creates a new file the same way: owner root,
# mode 0640, group the run user's)
#   cp vlbaweather.env.example /etc/vlbaweather.env
#   chgrp <run user's group> /etc/vlbaweather.env && chmod 0640 /etc/vlbaweather.env
# and rerun the installer (deploy/install-cron.sh, or deploy/install.sh under systemd) after
# changing WEATHER_OUT_DIR or WEATHER_CACHE_DIR (it creates the directories). Set those two
# in /etc/vlbaweather.env only: the installer runs as root and refuses paths from a file the
# run user can write (~<run user>/vlbaweather.env).
# Everything is optional and every line here is commented out, so the defaults apply:
# deploy/install-cron.sh then writes the output directory it chose into /etc/vlbaweather.env
# (/var/www/localhost/htdocs/vlbaweather on Gentoo, /var/www/html/vlbaweather elsewhere),
# deploy/install.sh writes its choice into the unit.
# The cron wrapper's own settings (WEATHER_LOG, WEATHER_PYTHON, WEATHER_TIMEOUT,
# WEATHER_GENERATOR) go in these files too; deploy/vlbaweather-cron.sh describes them.
# Values are checked at startup: a malformed number, a value outside the range given with it,
# or a switch other than 1/0, true/false, yes/no or on/off stops the generator (exit status 2).
#
# ABSOLUTELY CRUCIAL: set WEATHER_USER_AGENT with YOUR REAL E-MAIL ADDRESS (see below).
# With a placeholder such as you@example.org left in, OpenStreetMap blocks every map tile
# request (the maps show an "Access blocked" tile) and the generator refuses to start.
# (A '%' is literal in an EnvironmentFile; only in a unit's Environment= line it must be '%%'.)

# ---- sites -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# With neither variable set, the built-in list is used: the ten VLBA antennas, then the VLA and
# the GBT (vlba/sites.py: BR FD HN KP LA MK NL OV PT SC VLA GBT, the codes in lower case as
# slugs). Name other
# sites (a subset for tests, or another array) in one of two ways (the first that is set
# wins; --sites on the command line beats both):
# 1. WEATHER_SITES: slug|Display name|lat|lon[|tz] entries separated by ';', e.g.
#    pt|Pie Town, NM|34.3010|-108.1192|America/Denver;la|Los Alamos, NM|35.7751|-106.2456
# 2. WEATHER_SITES_FILE: a file with the same entries, one per line, '#' comments allowed;
#    an absolute path, e.g. /etc/vlbaweather/sites. An
#    unreadable or malformed file is a startup error (exit status 2), never skipped.
# slug = [A-Za-z0-9_-], unique without regard to case (used in file names and #site-<slug>
# anchors). Lat/lon in decimal degrees, US longitudes negative: a site whose map reaches no
# US state or territory, or whose map would cross the 180th meridian, is a startup error.
# tz (optional) = IANA time zone, used for local times whenever NWS /points metadata (the
# usual source of the zone) is unavailable; without it such times fall back to UTC. An
# unknown zone name is a startup error. The NWS grid, zones, time zone, alert areas and maps
# follow the sites; a site from these variables has no antenna code or elevation (the
# built-in entries carry both; the observation checks use the elevation).
# WEATHER_SITES=
# WEATHER_SITES_FILE=

# ---- paths -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# In these paths and VLBA_STATE_FILE a leading ~ is the run user's home; a $VARIABLE is
# refused (write the path out). The cache directory, the lock file and the state file must
# lie outside WEATHER_OUT_DIR, which the web server publishes whole, and WEATHER_OUT_DIR
# outside the cache, or the generator refuses to start. Under systemd only the output and
# cache directories are writable: keep the lock and state files in the cache directory.
# Where index.html, status.json and radar_<slug>_<theme>.png are written (Apache serves it).
# Without it the generator writes ./out; the installers fill it in: Gentoo's DocumentRoot
# gives /var/www/localhost/htdocs/vlbaweather, Debian/RHEL /var/www/html/vlbaweather.
# WEATHER_OUT_DIR=/var/www/localhost/htdocs/vlbaweather
# Disk cache: NWS JSON, zone shapes, basemap composites, last radar frames, the GLM state,
# the run lock. Separate from local-weather-awareness's cache.
# WEATHER_CACHE_DIR=~/.cache/vlbaweather
# Single-instance lock file (default: <WEATHER_CACHE_DIR>/run.lock).
# WEATHER_LOCK_FILE=

# ---- page ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Browser tab title and masthead. Empty = the default, "VLBA, VLA and GBT weather".
# WEATHER_TITLE=VLBA, VLA and GBT weather
# The page's public address, shown in the footer, e.g. https://<your host>/vlbaweather/.
# Empty: no address shown.
# WEATHER_PAGE_URL=
# Page auto-reload interval in seconds (minimum 30). The generator runs every 30 minutes
# (cron / timer); a reload every 5 minutes shows a new run's page within 5 minutes of its
# writing. The page reloads itself with JavaScript, keeping open sections and the scroll
# position; a <noscript> meta refresh does the same for browsers without JavaScript.
# WEATHER_REFRESH_SECONDS=300
# Minutes after which a page that was not regenerated says so by itself: a banner at the top
# ("generated N min ago and is no longer being updated") and greyed verdicts, judged by the
# visitor's clock; status.json carries the same limit as stale_after_s, for a monitor to
# compare with its "generated". 75 = two and a half run intervals of 30 min: one failed or
# late run raises nothing, a second one missed does. Raise it with a longer run interval
# (10..1440).
# VLBA_PAGE_STALE_MIN=75
# "us" = °F/mph primary with metric in small print; "metric" = the reverse.
# WEATHER_UNITS=us
# Rows in the hourly table.
# WEATHER_HOURLY_HOURS=24
# Day/night periods in the 7-day strip (NWS returns 14).
# WEATHER_FORECAST_PERIODS=14
# Precipitation probability (percent) at/above which a table cell is highlighted (the
# verdict's own threshold is VLBA_NWS_PRECIP_MAX below).
# WEATHER_POP_HIGHLIGHT_PCT=30
# Show the sunrise/sunset/twilight row per site (1/0).
# WEATHER_SHOW_SUN=1

# ---- HTTP ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# api.weather.gov REQUIRES a User-Agent, and NWS asks for a contact in it. The default
# names only the project, so a deployment should add its operator's e-mail address after
# the project URL, separated by "; ", e.g.
#   vlbaweather (+https://github.com/kirxkirx/vlbaweather; you@example.org)
# with you@example.org REPLACED BY YOUR REAL E-MAIL ADDRESS. This is crucial: without a real
# address the OpenStreetMap tiles cannot be fetched (OSM blocks User-Agents with placeholder
# contacts such as you@example.org or CONTACT_EMAIL, and the generator refuses to start).
# local-weather-awareness's env file does not reach this service: put the address here.
# The same User-Agent, with your contact, goes to every host the run fetches from.
# WEATHER_USER_AGENT=vlbaweather (+https://github.com/kirxkirx/vlbaweather)
# Per-request timeout in seconds (1..120).
# WEATHER_HTTP_TIMEOUT=25
# Retries after a network error or a 5xx response (0..5). A 429, or a 503 with Retry-After,
# is not retried: that host is skipped for the rest of the run.
# WEATHER_HTTP_RETRIES=2
# Network time budget of one run, in seconds (10..3600). Once it is spent, every further
# fetch is skipped and the page is built from last-good copies (labelled stale); a host that
# times out or refuses connections after its retries is skipped for the rest of that run.
# A run fetches twelve sites, the radar frames and the GLM granules of half an hour (their own
# cap is VLBA_GLM_TIMEOUT), hence more than local-weather-awareness's 90 s: a cold first run
# (tiles for every site and theme, the NWS station and zone lookups) took 3.5 min with ten
# sites on 2026-10-12, a warm one takes a minute or two. Keep it well below the wrapper's
# 600 s timeout and the unit's TimeoutStartSec=10min.
# WEATHER_RUN_BUDGET=240

# ---- NWS caching (seconds, 0..604800): _TTL = re-fetch after; _MAX_STALE = keep showing the
# last good copy until it is this old.
# /points metadata (grid, zones, time zone, station list) almost never changes (0..31622400,
# 366 days); an answer without a forecast grid is asked for again within an hour.
# WEATHER_POINTS_TTL=604800
# 7-day forecast. The forecast TTLs stay below the 30-minute run interval, so every
# scheduled run fetches the forecasts afresh (a run started by hand in between reuses them).
# WEATHER_FORECAST_TTL=1500
# WEATHER_FORECAST_MAX_STALE=7200
# Hourly forecast.
# WEATHER_HOURLY_TTL=1500
# WEATHER_HOURLY_MAX_STALE=7200
# Station observations.
# WEATHER_OBS_TTL=600
# WEATHER_OBS_MAX_STALE=3600
# (The raw gridpoint forecast the verdict uses has its own VLBA_GRIDPOINT_* lines below.)

# ---- alerts ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# States, territories and marine areas whose active alerts are fetched (api.weather.gov
# ?area= list). auto = every state, territory and NWS marine area whose bounding box touches
# a site's map (the run log names them; for the VLBA list that includes HI, VI and PR and
# the marine areas around them). Codes listed with auto are added (auto,PK); without auto
# only the listed codes are queried, e.g. NM,TX, and a site in a state the list leaves out
# is reported in the log and in status.json (its alerts are not fetched, so its warning
# check reads "no data").
# WEATHER_ALERT_AREAS=auto
# The alert feed is fetched every run; on failure the last good copy is shown this long:
# 40 min covers one failed run at the 30-minute interval, so a failed fetch does not drop a
# warning in effect; after two the alerts read "unknown". 0..86400: no warning that cannot
# be confirmed gone hangs on for more than a day. More than 3 h is reported as a run problem
# on every run while the warning check is on.
# WEATHER_ALERTS_MAX_STALE=2400
# Opacity (0..255) of the alert-area fill on the maps (outline is always solid).
# WEATHER_ALERT_FILL_ALPHA=55
# Per-event colour overrides for the maps and the page: 'Event Name=#rrggbb' entries
# separated by ';'. Event names as NWS sends them, matched case-insensitively. They replace
# the built-in palette (the NWS chart, except inland flood products, which are drawn in reds
# instead of the chart's greens). A malformed entry is a startup error. Example:
# WEATHER_ALERT_COLORS=Flood Watch=#E53935;Red Flag Warning=#FF1493

# ---- radar map -------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Map width AND height, statute miles, centred on each site (10..1000).
# WEATHER_MAP_MILES=100
# Output width in pixels (200..2000); height follows the Mercator aspect (about square).
# WEATHER_MAP_PX=600
# Basemap tile zoom (5..12): 9 = about 1.5 km/px before downsampling. A map may take at most
# 256 tiles, or the generator refuses to start: the VLBA sites' 100-mile maps take up to 16
# at zoom 9, 49 at 10, 182 at 11.
# WEATHER_TILE_ZOOM=9
# Tile URL templates ({z}/{x}/{y}); default OpenStreetMap standard tiles for both themes
# (CARTO's free rasters now return an "API KEY REQUIRED" tile). Any {z}/{x}/{y} raster works.
# WEATHER_TILE_URL_DARK=https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png
# WEATHER_TILE_URL_LIGHT=https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png
# Dark theme: invert the lightness (hue kept) of each tile that comes out light; 0 = use
# the tiles as served (for a genuinely dark tile set).
# WEATHER_TILE_DARK_INVERT=1
# Set to 0 to skip the MRMS radar layer entirely (maps still show basemap + alerts). The
# sites outside the MRMS grid (Mauna Kea, St. Croix) have their own switch, VLBA_ISLAND_RADAR.
# WEATHER_RADAR=1
# Echoes below this reflectivity (dBZ) are not drawn (the verdict's own threshold is
# VLBA_RADAR_DBZ below).
# WEATHER_RADAR_DBZ_MIN=5
# Opacity (0..255) of the radar layer.
# WEATHER_RADAR_ALPHA=170
# How many 2-minute frames to look back for the newest available MRMS image (0..30).
# WEATHER_RADAR_MAX_BACK=10
# Reuse the last good frame (labelled STALE) this long (0..604800) if the archive is
# unreachable: 40 min, one failed 30-minute run (the verdict stops trusting a frame after
# VLBA_RADAR_STALE seconds).
# WEATHER_RADAR_MAX_STALE=2400
# strftime pattern of the MRMS composite-reflectivity PNG archive (Iowa Environmental
# Mesonet): http:// or https://, the file name holding %Y %m %d %H and %M (the frame's time
# to the minute).
# WEATHER_MRMS_ARCHIVE=https://mesonet.agron.iastate.edu/archive/data/%Y/%m/%d/GIS/mrms/lcref_%Y%m%d%H%M.png

# ---- road closures ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Not part of this page: road closures are off (WEATHER_ROADS stays off, and the library's
# other road variables, WEATHER_ROADS_*, WEATHER_LSR*, WEATHER_NMROADS_*, are not used here).
# Set to on, WEATHER_ROADS or WEATHER_LSR is ignored with a warning in the log.

# ---- misc ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DEBUG logging to stderr/journal (same as --verbose).
# WEATHER_VERBOSE=0

# ---- VLBA: the verdict -----------------------------------------------------------------
# GOOD / BAD per antenna with the weather cuts of ttustatus as the boundary: BAD when any
# check below is bad, GOOD otherwise; a check without usable data counts as "unknown", never
# as clear (the page then shows "GOOD?" naming the missing inputs). Sun altitude is
# information only, never a check. A plain http:// URL for a verdict source
# (WEATHER_MRMS_ARCHIVE, VLBA_MRMS_URL, VLBA_MRMS_CONUS_MIRROR, VLBA_NWSRADAR_URL,
# VLBA_METAR_URL) is accepted, for a local mirror, but reported as a run problem on every run
# while its check is on: without TLS its data can be altered in transit.
#
# NWS warning products that make the weather BAD while one is in effect over the antenna
# (the alert's polygon contains it, or a zone product lists its zones), counted from
# VLBA_VETO_ONSET_LEAD seconds before the alert's onset to its end. Separated by commas only
# (no quotes, no trailing # comment), matched without regard to case; a name holding ; # a
# quote or a backslash, or only digits or a switch word, stops the generator. A name the
# built-in colour table does not know is kept (NWS renames products now and then) but
# reported as a run problem on every run, and so is VLBA_VETO_EVENTS=none, which switches
# the warning check off (an empty value does too under systemd, but the cron wrapper skips
# empty values, so use "none"). Default:
# ttustatus's three (Tornado, Dust Storm, High Wind Warning) plus the other damaging-wind
# products (Extreme Wind, Hurricane, Typhoon, Tropical Storm) and the snowstorm products.
# VLBA_VETO_EVENTS=Tornado Warning,Dust Storm Warning,High Wind Warning,Extreme Wind Warning,Hurricane Warning,Typhoon Warning,Tropical Storm Warning,Blizzard Warning,Winter Storm Warning,Ice Storm Warning,Snow Squall Warning,Lake Effect Snow Warning
# Seconds before a warning's onset from which it counts (0..604800): ttustatus's 15 min plus
# the 30-minute run interval, so the page is BAD at least 15 min before every onset.
# VLBA_VETO_ONSET_LEAD=2700
# Radar: an echo of at least VLBA_RADAR_DBZ dBZ (-30..80) within VLBA_RADAR_KM km (<= 300) of
# the antenna in VLBA_RADAR_TRIGGER_AFTER consecutive radar frames (1..6; each frame counts
# once, a frame seen again or reused after a failed fetch is no new evidence) makes the
# weather BAD; the check stays bad VLBA_RADAR_HOLD seconds (0..604800) after the last
# confirmed hit (with runs 30 min apart, that only matters for runs started by hand).
# Where the newest frame shows an echo in a ring, the run also fetches the frames just before
# it (VLBA_RADAR_TRIGGER_AFTER - 1 of them), so one run confirms the echo by itself: an echo
# not confirmed is "unconfirmed" when an earlier frame was read and showed none, "no data"
# when the frames before could not be read. A frame whose ring holds the same data as the
# frame counted last is no new observation (the composites are written every 2 min, a radar
# completes a scan every 4.5-10 min): the run passes over it for an older one, never one more
# than VLBA_RADAR_STALE before the newest frame (and the verdict counts a repeat of the scan
# counted last as nothing new). VLBA_RADAR_STALE (<= 604800) must therefore be at least
# 360 + 600 x (VLBA_RADAR_TRIGGER_AFTER - 1) seconds (960 for the default 2, 1560 for 3), or
# the generator refuses to start. A radar frame older than VLBA_RADAR_STALE seconds is
# "no data" for the verdict, never "clear". The source is MRMS (CONUS) or the NWS composite
# (islands).
# VLBA_RADAR_KM=30
# VLBA_RADAR_DBZ=20
# VLBA_RADAR_TRIGGER_AFTER=2
# VLBA_RADAR_HOLD=900
# VLBA_RADAR_STALE=1200
# Radar coverage (1/0): the radar frames write "no radar covers this cell" like "no echo", so
# with this on each run also reads MRMS's own file of the frame's time from the NOAA Open
# Data bucket below (CONUS: SeamlessHSR, about 1.3 MB; the islands' composites:
# MergedBaseReflectivityQC, 20-60 KB) and counts the ring's cells that no radar covers. A
# ring without an echo is "clear" only while radar covers at least VLBA_RADAR_MIN_COVERAGE
# percent (0..100) of it; less coverage, or coverage that could not be read, is "no data"
# (an echo counts either way). Off: a ring without an echo is "clear", the detail saying
# coverage was not checked.
# VLBA_RADAR_COVERAGE=1
# VLBA_RADAR_MIN_COVERAGE=90
# Base URL (http:// or https://) of the MRMS bucket; <domain>/<product>/<YYYYMMDD>/ follows.
# VLBA_MRMS_URL=https://noaa-mrms-pds.s3.amazonaws.com
# NOAA's feed to that bucket is not real time by contract: on 2026-11-10 its files came 25-30
# min late in every domain (the radar check read "no data" at every site), on 2026-09-11 it
# stalled for an hour. A CONUS file the bucket lacks is then read from this mirror: a
# strftime pattern of the file's URL (%Y %m %d %H %M %S, all six in the file name, which
# must be MRMS's SeamlessHSR_00.00 file's; the directory nothing finer than %H, listed hour
# by hour when the file of the frame's time is missing there too). The default is IEM's
# archive, which gets the same files from NCEP directly (byte for byte the bucket's) and
# had them within minutes on 2026-09-11 and 2026-11-10 (the 94 min of 2026-10-07 reached
# neither in time). The value none switches the mirror off; an empty value cannot. Nobody
# mirrors the Hawaii and Caribbean files.
# VLBA_MRMS_CONUS_MIRROR=https://mtarchive.geol.iastate.edu/%Y/%m/%d/mrms/ncep/SeamlessHSR/SeamlessHSR_00.00_%Y%m%d-%H%M%S.grib2.gz
# When no source has a file within 5 min of the frame's time, an older file stands in: the
# newest one at most this many seconds before the frame's time (0..3600; one hour since
# 2026-11-10). It keeps the radar check alive while NOAA's feed is late, above all at the
# islands, which have no mirror (on 2026-11-10 their newest file was 12-24 min older than
# the frame), at a price: a radar that failed after that file reads "clear" until a newer
# file shows it (St. Croix's ring has one radar, TJUA). Such a check is marked stale, its
# detail says so, the footer pill turns amber and status.json lists the coverage as stale.
# Never for a frame of unknown time or a reused copy, nor while the files near the frame's
# time could not all be listed or read. 0 keeps the strict rule: no echo is "clear" only on
# MRMS's data of the frame's time.
# VLBA_RADAR_COVERAGE_MAX_LAG=3600
# Forecast (the NWS raw gridpoint, this hour or next): BAD when the sky cover, the
# precipitation probability or the thunder probability (percent, <= 100) exceeds the value.
# A negative value switches that check off (e.g. VLBA_NWS_CLOUD_MAX=-1: clouds do not
# bother a radio antenna; the cut is kept because ttustatus has it).
# VLBA_NWS_CLOUD_MAX=60
# VLBA_NWS_PRECIP_MAX=20
# VLBA_NWS_THUNDER_MAX=15
# Winter precipitation: BAD when the forecast for this hour or next has snowfall or ice
# accumulation, or snow / sleet / freezing rain with a coverage of likely or more (1/0).
# VLBA_NWS_WINTER=1
# A forecast whose NWS update is older than this many minutes (1..10080) is "no data" for the
# verdict. ttustatus uses 150 min; NWS offices update their grids only a few times a day
# (2 to 6 h apart on 2026-10-08), so the VLBA page allows 12 h.
# VLBA_NWS_STALE_MIN=720
# Caching of the raw gridpoint forecast (seconds): re-fetch after _TTL; keep showing the
# last good copy until _MAX_STALE, like the NWS products above (0..604800; the TTL below the
# 30-minute run interval: every scheduled run fetches it afresh).
# VLBA_GRIDPOINT_TTL=1500
# VLBA_GRIDPOINT_MAX_STALE=7200
# Observed relative humidity (percent, <= 100) above which the weather is BAD; negative =
# the check is off, the default: ttustatus's 95 % protects an optical dome from
# condensation, which does not concern a radio antenna (VLBA_OBS_HUMIDITY_MAX=95 restores it).
# VLBA_OBS_HUMIDITY_MAX=-1
# BAD while the station (below) reports precipitation now (present weather, the report text,
# the raw METAR's weather groups, or precipitation in the last hour). When its latest record
# says nothing about the weather, or is over 90 min old: the newest report with weather of
# the last 2 h of this station or of the next three qualifying ones or, when NWS has none
# with weather of the last 90 min, of their METARs from aviationweather.gov (VLBA_METAR_URL
# below; the nearer station on a tie, then NWS's copy of the same report; it moves only this
# check, not wind or humidity; a report without a temperature counts); n/a only when none of
# them ever reports weather (mesonet, RAWS: judged by their last 12 reports), "no data"
# otherwise, also while a newer NWS report could not be read (aviationweather.gov out of
# reach is no such doubt: only named when no report answers) (1/0).
# VLBA_OBS_PRECIP=1
# The observation checks use the nearest station of NWS's list for the antenna's grid cell
# that lies within this many km of the antenna and within this many metres of its
# elevation and has a recent report (Kitt Peak: QSBA3, its nearest station lies 1213 m
# lower; Brewster: CJDW, whose precipitation check reads KOMK's reports, as CJDW reports no
# weather). Where no station of the list qualifies (Mauna Kea, Pie Town, the VLA, the GBT:
# its nearest, Elkins' KEKN, lies at 50.3 km, just beyond the default limit), or
# none reports weather (the precipitation check at Kitt Peak), the checks are "n/a" and the
# verdict rests on the forecast, radar, GLM and warnings.
# VLBA_OBS_MAX_KM=50
# VLBA_OBS_MAX_DELEV_M=1000
# The precipitation check reads a report up to 2 h old. NWS's API serves some stations'
# METARs late (St. Croix's TISX about 40 min, 2026-11-10: at the :30 runs its newest report
# there was 97 min old), so when NWS has no report with weather of the last 90 min, the
# stations' METARs are read from aviationweather.gov's data API (base URL below; one
# request, only then; a station that never reports weather, Kitt Peak's QSBA3, makes it ask
# every run, answered 204: nothing), and the newest report counts. A base URL: no query,
# fragment, user name or password. none switches that off; an empty value cannot.
# VLBA_METAR_URL=https://aviationweather.gov/api/data/metar
# Wind (mph): BAD when the observed wind or gust (from a station the two rules above admit),
# or the forecast wind or gust this hour or next, reaches this speed; the VLBA antennas
# stow in high wind (ttustatus has no wind cut). 0 = no wind check.
# VLBA_WIND_MPH=45
# Where the holds (radar, GLM), the radar streak and where the next GLM window starts live
# between runs, written atomically once per run. Empty: <WEATHER_CACHE_DIR>/vlba_state.json
# (see the paths section).
# VLBA_STATE_FILE=

# ---- VLBA: GLM lightning ---------------------------------------------------------------
# GOES GLM flashes of the last minutes, drawn on the maps and used by the verdict (1/0).
# Needs numpy and h5py (or netCDF4) for the run user's Python (Gentoo: dev-python/numpy
# dev-python/h5py; Debian: python3-numpy python3-h5py; RHEL: the same names with dnf);
# without them the page says lightning is unavailable and the lightning check reads
# "no data" at every antenna (GOOD? at best); the rest works. Day and night.
# VLBA_GLM=1
# A flash within this many km (<= 500) of the antenna makes the weather BAD: the check is bad
# for VLBA_GLM_HOLD seconds (60..604800) after the last flash (a flash of the window that is
# older than that no longer counts; VLBA_GLM=0 switches lightning off).
# VLBA_GLM_KM=50
# VLBA_GLM_HOLD=1800
# Minutes of 20-second granules fetched per run (1..60; one more minute of overlap with the
# previous run is added, for granules published late, and a late run reaches back to the
# newest granule the previous one examined in one piece, a run without that record the whole
# way: as far as VLBA_GLM_HOLD (or 15 min, or the window, whichever is longest: every
# granule whose flashes still count, nothing older); a granule that does not arrive is
# fetched again, first of all, by the next run, once, unless its flashes are past
# VLBA_GLM_HOLD by then), and use only every Nth granule (1..30; 2 halves the traffic).
# 45-75 MB per run (about 185 granules) with both satellites and the 30-minute window. Keep
# the window equal to the run interval (cron / timer): a shorter one, or a stride above 1,
# leaves part of each interval unexamined; a longer one fetches each granule again on every
# run within it.
# VLBA_GLM_WINDOW_MIN=30
# VLBA_GLM_STRIDE=1
# The satellites: bucket=sub-satellite longitude (degrees, east positive) entries separated
# by ';', the bucket being the NOAA Open Data bucket on AWS that holds the GLM-L2-LCFA
# granules (anonymous HTTPS). GOES-East (GOES-19, 75.2 W) and GOES-West (GOES-18, 137.0 W).
# Each antenna uses the satellites that cover it (VLBA_GLM_MAX_ARC); the flashes of both
# are combined. With GLM on, every built-in antenna must be covered by a listed satellite
# (mind the minus signs), or the generator refuses to start. Switch the layer off with the
# setting VLBA_GLM=0, not by emptying this.
# VLBA_GLM_BUCKETS=noaa-goes19=-75.2;noaa-goes18=-137.0
# A satellite covers a site that lies within this many degrees of arc (0..90) of its
# sub-satellite point. GLM's field of view ends about 62 deg away diagonally (in the granules
# of 2026-10-14 GOES-18 recorded nothing near the GBT, 64.9 deg away). Mauna Kea: West only;
# St. Croix, Hancock and the GBT: East only; the other eight, Brewster at 61.6 deg from
# GOES-East and North Liberty at 58.4 deg from GOES-West included, both.
# VLBA_GLM_MAX_ARC=62
# Cap on the whole GLM fetch of a run, seconds (5..3600; keep it inside WEATHER_RUN_BUDGET;
# 184 granules took about 25 s with 4 downloads on 2026-10-14), and parallel granule
# downloads (1..16).
# VLBA_GLM_TIMEOUT=90
# VLBA_GLM_WORKERS=4
# A satellite's lightning window counts as complete only when its newest granule ends at
# most this many seconds before the run (60..1800), no more than 2 min in a row of it is
# missing and the listing is whole; otherwise the check says "no data" unless a flash was
# found. A healthy feed publishes a granule 5-75 s after its end.
# VLBA_GLM_MAX_LAG=300

# ---- VLBA: radar outside the MRMS grid -------------------------------------------------
# Mauna Kea and St. Croix lie outside the MRMS CONUS grid: with this on (1/0) their maps
# and radar check use the NWS radar composites (radar.weather.gov's own data, MRMS-derived,
# a frame every 2 min) from the WMS below, decoded from the server's colours to dBZ and
# drawn in the same palette as the MRMS maps. Off: those sites show no radar and their
# radar check is "off". While on, VLBA_RADAR_KM must be at most half the map width (the
# composites are fetched for the map only).
# VLBA_ISLAND_RADAR=1
# Base URL (http:// or https://) of the NWS radar WMS; the hawaii and carib workspaces and
# their ows endpoints are appended.
# VLBA_NWSRADAR_URL=https://opengeo.ncep.noaa.gov/geoserver
# Reuse the last good composite (labelled stale) this long (seconds, 0..604800) when the
# server is unreachable: 40 min, one failed 30-minute run; the verdict's own freshness limit
# is VLBA_RADAR_STALE.
# VLBA_NWSRADAR_MAX_STALE=2400
